Subject: engine: the element profile records the storage kind of the arrays a site accessed (QJS_PACKED_ARRAYS)

What this does
  JSAotProfPc gains `packed_kinds`: bit 0 when a JS_CLASS_ARRAY receiver of int32 elements was seen
  at the site, bit 1 for float64, bit 2 for a generic (JSValue) storage. js_aot_prof_packed records
  it from p->arr_kind at the register VM's element read and at its element store, next to the
  element-value kind the profiler already keeps, and the profile line carries it as ` pk=<n>` when
  it is nonzero. A site that saw no array receiver (or a typed array) records nothing.

  A JS_CLASS_ARRAY element store inside the count is recorded as taken: a packed array has no hole
  at such an index, so the store cannot grow the array or run a setter, and a site that only did
  that is where a packed region belongs.

Why we need it
  The element-value kind (`elem=`) says what a load or store moved, not what the array held. A site
  that reads numbers out of a float64 array and a site that writes numbers into an int32 array look
  alike there, and compiled code cannot tell whether a typed load on the raw storage is possible.
  The storage kind is what decides it, and it has to come from the profiled run.

Hazards
  A build without the profiler compiles none of this: the store site's record is a macro that
  expands to nothing, and the read sites' record is behind AOT_PROF_ELEM's own guard, so the
  register VM's element paths are the same code as before. In a profiling build a store pays one
  inline test of the function's profile pointer and the runtime switch.
  The byte is profile data only: the register VM and the generated code take no decision from it
  without testing the kind of each receiver at run time, so a profile that is stale or from another
  build can cost a miss, never correctness.
  A profile written before the field existed has no `pk=` and records no storage kind, so no
  compiled region forms from it; there is no version bump.

Tests: the profile of .local/kit/packed2.js and matmul carries pk=1, pk=2 and pk=4 at the sites
whose receivers are int32, float64 and generic; the flag-off engine's profile lines are unchanged
apart from the field, and the register VM's own behaviour is untouched (.local/kit/packed1.js,
packed2.js, matmul, sieve equal).
